      "assistant": {
        "Response": {
          "message_id": "fa2d0319-431a-46e0-9bf9-c471a417bf19",
          "content": "\n\nBookStack is a free, open-source, self-hosted wiki platform. It organizes content into a hierarchy: Shelves > Books > Chapters > Pages. You can attach files (like PDFs) to pages, and it has a full REST API for everything. It runs in Docker with a MySQL backend. No license, no limits, no cloud dependency.\n\nYour plan makes sense: use BookStack locally as the development stand-in for Confluence, build and test the full sync pipeline against it, then swap the connector to point at Confluence Cloud when you're ready for production. The two systems are different APIs, but the pattern is the same: list pages, grab PDF attachments, ingest them.\n\nHere's how the mapping would work:\n\n| Concept | BookStack | Confluence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Top level | Shelf | Space |\n| Group | Book | Parent page |\n| Category | Chapter | Child page |\n| Content | Page + attachments | Page + attachments |\n| API auth | Token-based | Basic auth + API token |\n\nSo your \"Closing Documents\" category would be a Book (or Chapter) in BookStack with PDFs attached to pages inside it. The connector pulls those attachments and feeds them into the same ingest pipeline we already have.\n\nThe work would be:\n\n1. Add BookStack container to the compose stack (BookStack + MySQL, both lightweight)\n2. Write a BookStack connector (similar to the Confluence one but different API)\n3. Build and test the full sync flow locally\n4. Later, swap in the Confluence connector when you move to cloud\n\nWant me to get started on this?"
        }
      },
