          "content": "\n\nGood challenge. Using Bedrock to classify every document on upload would be expensive and slow, especially at scale. Here's the smarter approach:\n\n**For text extraction and parsing:** Pure local libraries, no AI needed.\n- `pypdf` for PDFs (already using this)\n- `python-docx` for Word docs (already using this)\n- These are free, fast, and run locally\n\n**For auto-categorization:** Use a tiered approach instead of throwing everything at Bedrock.\n\n1. **First pass: Rule-based (free, instant)** - Expand the current keyword matcher to look at more of the document text, the filename, and structural patterns. This handles 80% of cases. A closing disclosure always says \"Closing Disclosure\" on page 1. HOA bylaws always say \"Bylaws.\" An appraisal always has \"UNIFORM RESIDENTIAL APPRAISAL REPORT.\"\n\n2. **Second pass: Local NLP (free, fast)** - For docs that land in \"general,\" use a lightweight local classifier. Something like `sentence-transformers` with a small model (runs on CPU, no API calls) to compare the document text against category descriptions using cosine similarity.\n\n3. **Bedrock only for Ask/Q&A** - Keep Bedrock for what it's good at: answering natural language questions. That's one API call per question, not per document.\n\nSo the cost structure would be:\n- Upload 92 docs: $0 (all local processing)\n- Ask a question: ~$0.001 per question (Haiku is cheap)\n\nWant me to build it this way? Beef up the rule-based classifier first, then add the local embedding fallback for the edge cases?"
